# value_place_once.tkr — the COL-F0a VALUE-regime fixture (crumb 0021, D56). `main.tks` opens a
# collection region (`region_new(region_root())`, the collection's OWN region) and `place`s N large
# `big::Big` value-structs into it, one per slot, reading each back by copy (`teko::mem::read`) to
# confirm the value landed, and records each stable pointer as an address in a fixed `[]i64` index.
# It then FORCES a grow: a new, larger index of_len'd and the pointers copied across. The residence
# contract is enforced AFTER the grow: every address in the grown index equals the original (the
# grow moved pointers, not values) AND each surviving pointer is DEREFERENCED (`word_ptr(...).__wrap`)
# to prove the placed value is intact — a `place` that had captured the incidental per-iteration loop
# region (the pre-D56 bug) would dangle here. The value region is created OUTSIDE the fill-loop and
# passed explicitly, so it outlives the loop's per-iteration temporaries. Exits 0 only if exactly N
# places happened, the read-back checksum holds, every pointer survived the grow unchanged, and the
# post-grow deref checksum equals the in-loop checksum. The compiler never `place`s a large
# VALUE-struct itself, so this path is not fixpoint-exercised. Pinned to the C backend: the VALUE
# regime emits on the C route and the native leg honest-stops.

Feature: value_place_once — place writes a large value once; a grow copies pointers, never values

  Scenario: place_once_then_grow_index_copies_pointers_and_never_recopies_the_values
    Given env = ["TEKO_BACKEND=c"]
    When the program is built and run
    Then it exits 0
